1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,590
I think these dashboards are beautiful.

2
00:00:02,590 --> 00:00:05,612
The Home Assistant community turns out
cards

3
00:00:05,612 --> 00:00:08,633
that are genuinely stunning to look at.

4
00:00:08,633 --> 00:00:14,245
Bar Card, one of the most widely used
cards for animated energy bars,

5
00:00:14,245 --> 00:00:19,424
lost its repository on HACS in 2025 — no
official maintainer left.

6
00:00:19,424 --> 00:00:22,878
The result: thousands of dashboards with a
broken

7
00:00:22,878 --> 00:00:26,331
red card, overnight, with no warning to
anyone.

8
00:00:26,331 --> 00:00:29,353
'Beautiful' and 'maintained' are two
different things.

9
00:00:29,353 --> 00:00:34,532
And there's a second problem, and this one
is more serious: if

10
00:00:34,532 --> 00:00:39,712
you want to reach that dashboard from your
phone when you're away

11
00:00:39,712 --> 00:00:44,029
from home, it has to be exposed to the
internet.

12
00:00:44,029 --> 00:00:49,209
And it isn't just cards you're exposing —
it's your personal data.

13
00:00:49,209 --> 00:00:52,662
Your presence patterns, your cameras,
sometimes your locks.

14
00:00:52,662 --> 00:00:56,547
A poorly secured dashboard on a platform
reachable from

15
00:00:56,547 --> 00:01:00,000
outside is an open door into your house.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,130
Faced with those two problems —
maintenance that gives out, and

17
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:10,259
security that gets neglected — I made one
decision up front,

18
00:01:10,259 --> 00:01:15,389
and it's the one that defines the whole
project: build something

19
00:01:15,389 --> 00:01:20,518
simple and effective, that ships with as
much security as possible

20
00:01:20,518 --> 00:01:25,648
by default, not as an option you bolt on
later if

21
00:01:25,648 --> 00:01:28,446
you happen to think of it.

22
00:01:28,446 --> 00:01:33,575
In practice that means: as few external
dependencies I have no

23
00:01:33,575 --> 00:01:38,705
control over as possible, an architecture
designed from day one to

24
00:01:38,705 --> 00:01:43,834
be exposed to the internet without that
being a gamble, and

25
00:01:43,834 --> 00:01:48,964
an environment that lets me roll back
cleanly when something breaks

26
00:01:48,964 --> 00:01:54,093
— because it will break, sooner or later,
on any project.

27
00:01:54,093 --> 00:01:59,223
Concretely, that translates into a CI/CD
setup that lets one self-taught

28
00:01:59,223 --> 00:02:04,352
person — not a DevOps team, just someone
motivated — grow

29
00:02:04,352 --> 00:02:09,482
the product step by step, or simply
restore a previous version

30
00:02:09,482 --> 00:02:13,212
straight into HAOS when an update goes
wrong.

31
00:02:13,212 --> 00:02:18,808
We'll go deep on that pipeline in episode
6, but I wanted

32
00:02:18,808 --> 00:02:24,404
you to know, right here in episode 1, that
this safety net

33
00:02:24,404 --> 00:02:29,1000
exists and that it's part of the
foundation, not a side feature.

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,148
There's a third reason behind all this,
simpler to state but

35
00:02:38,148 --> 00:02:45,556
just as central: Home Assistant's native
interface is very complex.

36
00:02:45,556 --> 00:02:49,259
Powerful, yes — but complex.

37
00:02:49,259 --> 00:02:54,444
Between the configuration menus, the
entities, the

38
00:02:54,444 --> 00:02:59,630
areas, the nested dashboards, it's easy to

39
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:04,815
get lost, especially when you're starting
out.

40
00:03:04,815 --> 00:03:13,704
What Visio Sapiens sets out to do is
simplify that experience —

41
00:03:13,704 --> 00:03:22,593
not hide Home Assistant's power, but make
it reachable through a coherent

42
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:30,000
interface, designed for daily use rather
than for technical configuration.

43
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,381
So what is it, concretely?

44
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:35,238
Visio Sapiens is not a dashboard.

45
00:03:35,238 --> 00:03:40,000
It's a control-center-style interface,
sitting on top of Home Assistant.

46
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,429
The distinction matters.

47
00:03:41,429 --> 00:03:45,714
A dashboard is a collection of cards you
assemble.

48
00:03:45,714 --> 00:03:50,952
A control center is a coherent system,
with its own rendering

49
00:03:50,952 --> 00:03:56,190
engine, its own visual logic, its own
identity — and it

50
00:03:56,190 --> 00:04:00,476
simply goes to Home Assistant to fetch the
data.

51
00:04:00,476 --> 00:04:06,190
And that distinction has a very concrete
consequence, one you'll see at

52
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:11,905
work in every episode: the interface isn't
assembled by hand, it's generated.

53
00:04:11,905 --> 00:04:16,667
You describe your home once — the
language, the format,

54
00:04:16,667 --> 00:04:21,429
the rooms — and every screen is produced
from that

55
00:04:21,429 --> 00:04:25,714
description, then kept in step as the home
changes.

56
00:04:25,714 --> 00:04:30,476
Adding a room isn't drawing a new
dashboard: it's declaring

57
00:04:30,476 --> 00:04:34,762
a room, and letting the generator do the
rest.

58
00:04:34,762 --> 00:04:38,095
A dashboard you assemble by hand ages.

59
00:04:38,095 --> 00:04:40,952
A dashboard that's generated keeps up.

60
00:04:40,952 --> 00:04:46,667
And that leads to the single rule that
drives absolutely every technical

61
00:04:46,667 --> 00:04:52,381
decision in this project, the one you'll
meet again in every episode:

62
00:04:52,381 --> 00:04:56,667
Home Assistant is nothing more than a data
engine.

63
00:04:56,667 --> 00:04:59,1000
The interface is driven entirely by VSSP.

64
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,219
So here's the question that comes up
straight away:

65
00:05:04,219 --> 00:05:07,969
why not just use the native Lovelace
cards?

66
00:05:07,969 --> 00:05:14,062
They exist, they're maintained by the Home
Assistant core, they work perfectly well.

67
00:05:14,062 --> 00:05:21,094
Exactly — they work perfectly well for
what they are: a system of stacked cards.

68
00:05:21,094 --> 00:05:26,719
But the moment you want a coherent visual
identity, animations that respond

69
00:05:26,719 --> 00:05:32,344
in real time, a HUD that behaves like a
real system interface

70
00:05:32,344 --> 00:05:37,500
rather than a grid of widgets, you hit
Lovelace's glass ceiling.

71
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:42,188
So the project made a radical call: refuse
the native

72
00:05:42,188 --> 00:05:46,875
Lovelace cards, and build its own
rendering engine on top.

73
00:05:46,875 --> 00:05:50,156
With three exceptions, deliberate and
documented, because

74
00:05:50,156 --> 00:05:53,438
it would be stupid to reinvent what

75
00:05:53,438 --> 00:05:56,719
already works well: weather-forecast,
logbook, and apexcharts-card.

76
00:05:56,719 --> 00:06:01,875
Everything else — the sidebar, the HUD,
the gauges, the energy

77
00:06:01,875 --> 00:06:07,031
rows, the rooms — is the in-house engine,
which I maintain,

78
00:06:07,031 --> 00:06:11,719
through the same CI/CD pipeline I
mentioned a moment ago.

79
00:06:11,719 --> 00:06:16,406
No dependency on a community maintainer
who can vanish overnight.

80
00:06:16,406 --> 00:06:22,031
Now let's be honest about the price of
that choice, because there

81
00:06:22,031 --> 00:06:27,188
is one: by refusing the community cards, I
become the maintainer.

82
00:06:27,188 --> 00:06:32,344
If a gauge breaks, nobody else is coming
to fix it.

83
00:06:32,344 --> 00:06:37,031
That's a real cost, and I take it on for

84
00:06:37,031 --> 00:06:41,250
a precise reason: that cost is one I
control.

85
00:06:41,250 --> 00:06:45,469
When a community card disappears, I
control nothing —

86
00:06:45,469 --> 00:06:49,688
not the timing, not the decision, not the
migration.

87
00:06:49,688 --> 00:06:57,656
A bug in my own engine, I can fix that
same evening and ship it straight out.

88
00:06:57,656 --> 00:07:01,875
And there's a side effect I hadn't seen
coming.

89
00:07:01,875 --> 00:07:07,031
Once you write your own engine, you stop
piling up patches.

90
00:07:07,031 --> 00:07:12,188
The famous card-mod, with its CSS
selectors reaching into the shadow

91
00:07:12,188 --> 00:07:17,344
DOM of a card you don't control — it
works, sure,

92
00:07:17,344 --> 00:07:21,562
right up until the update that renames a
class.

93
00:07:21,562 --> 00:07:25,781
At that point there's no patch left to
write:

94
00:07:25,781 --> 00:07:30,000
there's CSS I wrote, sitting on HTML I
produced.

95
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,138
Here's what the full architecture looks
like.

96
00:07:34,138 --> 00:07:40,640
I'm showing it to you once, in full,
because it's the

97
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:47,143
mental map you'll need to follow every
episode that comes after.

98
00:07:47,143 --> 00:07:53,054
CORE: the system foundation — this is what
exposes the

99
00:07:53,054 --> 00:07:58,966
metrics, the machine state, the thing
everything else leans on.

100
00:07:58,966 --> 00:08:04,877
We'll come back to it in detail in episode
2.

101
00:08:04,877 --> 00:08:11,379
Room Engine: the logic that turns a room
definition into an

102
00:08:11,379 --> 00:08:17,291
interface — the heart of the dashboard
generator, episode 3.

103
00:08:17,291 --> 00:08:22,020
AI Layer: the layer that wires
intelligence into

104
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:26,158
the interface — the chatbot, the
automations.

105
00:08:26,158 --> 00:08:27,340
Episode 9.

106
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:33,842
Animation Engine, CSS Engine, JS Engine,
Theme Engine: the four layers

107
00:08:33,842 --> 00:08:40,345
that give Visio Sapiens its visual
identity and its behaviour —

108
00:08:40,345 --> 00:08:46,847
this is what makes a room feel like a
living interface

109
00:08:46,847 --> 00:08:52,167
rather than a Lovelace card wearing a nice
theme.

110
00:08:52,167 --> 00:08:58,670
Take just one thing away from this
diagram: every layer has

111
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:05,172
one precise responsibility, and none of
them does another one's job.

112
00:09:05,172 --> 00:09:13,448
That's what lets the project hold up over
time instead of turning into a

113
00:09:13,448 --> 00:09:21,724
plate of YAML spaghetti — and it's also
what makes the CI/CD pipeline possible:

114
00:09:21,724 --> 00:09:29,1000
you can only version and restore cleanly
what is structured in the first place.

115
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,032
Enough theory, let's look at the result.

116
00:09:33,032 --> 00:09:36,931
This is HOME, the landing screen of Visio
Sapiens.

117
00:09:36,931 --> 00:09:41,697
The sidebar — navigation between the
screens of the control center.

118
00:09:41,697 --> 00:09:47,762
The HUD row in the header: weather, clock,
alarm status, avatar — everything you

119
00:09:47,762 --> 00:09:53,394
want to take in at a glance as you walk
into the house.

120
00:09:53,394 --> 00:09:58,159
The energy row — production, consumption,
what the house is doing

121
00:09:58,159 --> 00:10:02,924
right now, not a history you have to go
digging for.

122
00:10:02,924 --> 00:10:07,256
The room modules — one screen per declared
room, all

123
00:10:07,256 --> 00:10:11,155
produced by the same generator, from the
same template.

124
00:10:11,155 --> 00:10:16,787
These aren't ten dashboards written ten
times: it's one template, and ten rooms.

125
00:10:16,787 --> 00:10:21,986
And the navigation rail, here, which
rebuilds itself when a room appears

126
00:10:21,986 --> 00:10:27,184
or disappears — because it's generated
too, not maintained by hand in

127
00:10:27,184 --> 00:10:31,516
some corner of YAML that you always end up
forgetting.

128
00:10:31,516 --> 00:10:37,148
And now the part I most enjoy showing,
because it makes all of

129
00:10:37,148 --> 00:10:42,780
this concrete: the repository tree, live,
side by side with what's on screen.

130
00:10:42,780 --> 00:10:47,545
This file — www/vssp/css/vssp.css — is
literally what paints that screen.

131
00:10:47,545 --> 00:10:52,310
Not a metaphor: every colour, every bit of
spacing you see

132
00:10:52,310 --> 00:10:56,643
on the right comes from that file on the
left.

133
00:10:56,643 --> 00:11:01,841
That's what having an in-house engine
means, rather than a theme laid

134
00:11:01,841 --> 00:11:07,040
over generic cards: the code and the
interface speak the same language

135
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:11,372
— and so they're versionable like a real
software project.

136
00:11:11,372 --> 00:11:15,704
And if I change a colour here, on the
left,

137
00:11:15,704 --> 00:11:19,603
it changes on the right at the next
deployment.

138
00:11:19,603 --> 00:11:23,935
On every screen at once, because there's
only one source.

139
00:11:23,935 --> 00:11:29,1000
Not ten cards to touch up one by one,
hoping you didn't miss any.

140
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,448
A quick aside before we wrap up, because
you're going

141
00:11:33,448 --> 00:11:36,897
to run into this in later episodes: the
project was

142
00:11:36,897 --> 00:11:39,655
originally called OSVision, and was
renamed to VSSP.

143
00:11:39,655 --> 00:11:44,138
You'll come across old file names and old
paths dating from that era.

144
00:11:44,138 --> 00:11:48,621
I'm mentioning it now for a simple reason:
a project's history leaves marks.

145
00:11:48,621 --> 00:11:52,069
A good project doesn't hide those marks,
it documents them.

146
00:11:52,069 --> 00:11:56,207
You'll see this renaming scar again later,
notably in the K3s bug

147
00:11:56,207 --> 00:11:59,1000
in episode 2 — and now you'll know why
it's there.

148
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:08,462
None of what you've just seen existed a
few months ago.

149
00:12:08,462 --> 00:12:16,923
quick list of 3-4 changelog milestones, to
be picked from Vision.md.

150
00:12:16,923 --> 00:12:23,846
This isn't a frozen project — it's a
system

151
00:12:23,846 --> 00:12:30,000
with a history, and it's still writing
one.

152
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,239
What to take away from this episode: Visio
Sapiens isn't a dashboard, it's

153
00:12:34,239 --> 00:12:38,152
a control center, built to be simple,
effective and secure by default.

154
00:12:38,152 --> 00:12:41,087
Home Assistant supplies the data, VSSP
supplies everything else

155
00:12:41,087 --> 00:12:44,022
— without depending on community cards
that can give

156
00:12:44,022 --> 00:12:46,957
out without warning, and with a pipeline
that lets

157
00:12:46,957 --> 00:12:49,891
you roll back when you need to, even
self-taught.

158
00:12:49,891 --> 00:12:54,783
In the next episode we go down a level: we
follow a single piece of

159
00:12:54,783 --> 00:12:59,674
data, from psutil on the host machine all
the way to an SVG gauge in

160
00:12:59,674 --> 00:13:03,913
your browser, with DevTools open so you
can watch every step go past.

161
00:13:03,913 --> 00:13:07,174
Subscribe, hit the bell, and tell me in
the comments:

162
00:13:07,174 --> 00:13:10,435
does your Home Assistant install still
look like a stack

163
00:13:10,435 --> 00:13:13,696
of Lovelace cards, or have you already
started climbing out?

164
00:13:13,696 --> 00:13:15,000
See you very soon.
